        card(2, "Euler's Totient Function: phi(n)", "Counting how many numbers on a clock face don't share any gears with the total hours.", "Euler's totient phi(n) counts integers from 1 to n coprime to n. If p is prime, phi(p) = p - 1. For n = p * q, phi(n) = (p - 1)(q - 1).", "For primes 3 and 7: n = 21, and phi(21) = (3 - 1)(7 - 1) = 2 * 6 = 12."),
        card(3, "Fermat & Miller-Rabin Primality", "Shaking a locked box: if it rattles a certain way, it's definitely not a solid stone.", "Fermat's Little Theorem: a^(p-1) = 1 (mod p). Miller-Rabin tests odd numbers with random bases; passing 40 rounds makes probability of being composite < 2^-80.", "Used by cryptographic libraries to generate safe 2048-bit prime numbers quickly.")
